# Research handoff: Idea 2 — a shareable, reversible KV cache (prefix-closed compression + score-aware index over an offloaded cache)

This is a complete, self-contained brief for a new Claude session (chat and Claude Code) with no history of the project. It contains the motivation, the definitions, the system, the prior art, the kill gates, and the first instructions. Read it fully before touching any code.

---

## 1. One-paragraph summary

Serving engines do not deploy KV-cache compression because every published method breaks the two things engines depend on: **prefix caching** (storing a shared prompt's KV once and reusing it across requests) and a **stable, rectangular memory layout** (every head the same length, so paged kernels and the scheduler stay simple). This project builds a cache that engines can actually share and recover. It has two pieces. (1) **Prefix-closed compression**: an eviction rule whose decisions depend only on a bounded lookahead and a fixed count, so the compressed cache of a shared prefix is identical for every request that starts with it; a cache hit returns exactly what a miss would build. (2) **A score-aware low-rank index over an offloaded cache**: the full KV lives in host memory, a compact index on the GPU decides which tokens to fetch each step, nothing is ever lost, and the index itself is prefix-closed by construction. CrossCov-U, the owner's existing Loki-style covariance-weighted key projection, is the index. The one-line claim: "A KV cache that engines can share and recover: prefix-closed, reversible, on stock kernels, with a score-aware index that makes offloading fast."

