## 2. Background the new session needs

### 2.1 What the owner already has
- **CrossCov-U**: a KV-selection method built on Loki (arXiv 2406.02542). It projects keys to a low-rank basis chosen by a cross-covariance criterion (score-aware, not plain PCA) and scores queries against the projected keys to select which tokens attend. Its known problem: on a GPU-resident cache it gives **no wall-clock speedup**, for the same reason Loki does not (Loki measured ~1.45x attention-only versus 2.6x theoretical, no end-to-end gain). SAKI (arXiv 2608.03228, Aug 2026) is near-duplicate prior art for the projection itself: a closed-form covariance-weighted rank-r score map that raised top-64 recall from 0.748 to 0.799 at r=32 over PCA, with no timing results. So the projection is not the contribution; the runtime and the sharing property are.
- **A harness from the previous project** (the `neymankv` repository on GitHub under PseudoHunt): capture hooks for pre- and post-RoPE keys, values and queries; a kvpress-based long-context evaluation pipeline (RULER, LooGLE) with paired bootstrap statistics; a decode engine with a per-token logit-bias path; synthetic test scaffolding. Reuse the capture, evaluation and statistics code. Ignore the eviction methods in it (they were null results).
- **A RoPE kernel-factorization repository** (github.com/PseudoHunt/rope-kv-compression) with RoPE handling utilities (inverse rotation, per-frequency analysis). Useful for the pre-RoPE index.

### 2.2 What the serving literature says (verified in the Oct 2026 survey)
- Compaction-style eviction drops prefix-hit rates to **0-3%**; IntentKV (arXiv 2606.09916) keeps 20.7% by masking evicted slots, but that does not free the memory.
- vLLM's prefix-caching RFC (#2614) hashes only complete blocks and warned that eviction "may randomly free a block in the middle of a prefix"; a later sparse-KV RFC (#12254) says token dropping is hard to combine with prefix caching, and a maintainer suggested waiting until a major lab adopts it.
- What shipped in 2026 avoids the conflict: TensorRT-LLM's TriAttention compacts **generated tokens only** and preserves the prompt; SGLang's HiSparse (arXiv 2608.07009) **offloads inactive KV to host memory instead of evicting**, with its PD-colocation example running with `--disable-radix-cache`; vLLM's hybrid KV manager and SGLang's unified radix cache (Aug 2026) combine prefix caching only with position-based sliding windows.
- Non-uniform per-head lengths are the second blocker: Tangram (arXiv 2606.06302) measured page reclaiming at up to 25% of prefill time, decode stragglers up to 1.7x, and 15-20% of each decode step in CPU re-planning. KVzap's authors state no pruning method had been integrated into major engines.
- Sparsity turns into throughput only when the full cache leaves HBM (SparseServe 2509.24626, HiSparse, SparDA 2606.04511, NOSA 2510.13602). Fathom (arXiv 2609.17652) beats Loki/SparQ/Double-Sparsity-style index scans by 1.67x, but **only when the index is offloaded**; it reports no gain when GPU-resident. Break-even for GPU-resident sparse decode is about 10x sparsity (arXiv 2605.24168).
- The survey's conclusion, which this project takes as its design brief: **prefix compatibility and reversibility are the real adoption criteria.**

