## 3. Piece 1: prefix-closed compression

### 3.1 Definition
Split the token sequence into blocks of B tokens. A compression policy is **prefix-closed with lookahead L** if, for every layer and KV head:
1. The keep/drop decision for a token in block b depends only on tokens up to end(b) + L.
2. Selection inside a block uses a fixed count (or a fixed threshold), never a ranking against the whole context.
3. Block b becomes final once tokens up to end(b) + L exist, and is never re-ranked.

Add **masked prefill**: once a block is final, every later token attends only to that block's kept entries. Tokens inside the lookahead window still see the block in full.

Condition 2 is the one that matters most. A global top-k breaks closure for any scorer, because whether token i survives depends on how many later tokens outscore it. That is why SnapKV, KVzip (2505.23416), Expected Attention (2510.00636) and TriAttention (2604.04921), as published, are not prefix-closed. StreamingLLM (position rule), DMS (2506.05345; decision at creation, executed 256 tokens later) and KVzap (2601.07891) in threshold mode already satisfy the property, but no paper names it or uses it for cross-request sharing.

### 3.2 The sharing proposition (to state and prove)
Assume a prefix-closed policy, masked prefill, block-aligned finalization and deterministic arithmetic. Then for any prompt P and any continuation X: the keep-masks of all blocks finalized within P are identical whether the model reads P alone or P + X; the stored keys and values at every position in P are identical; and a server that stores P's finalized compressed blocks plus P's dense unfinalized tail, then resumes prefill on X, produces exactly the same cache and logits as a server that prefills P + X from scratch.

Proof sketch: induction on position. Under causal attention, the hidden state at position t depends only on tokens up to t and on the masks of blocks finalized before t. By closure, each such mask depends only on tokens up to its own finalization point, which is earlier than t. So everything at positions up to |P| is a function of P alone. A radix-tree hit therefore returns what a miss would build. The unfinalized tail (last partial block plus the lookahead) stays dense, which mirrors how vLLM and SGLang already treat sliding-window layers (a dense trailing window, tombstones for missing slots).

### 3.3 The kernel-friendly design
Keep the **same count k per block for every head and layer**, while each head picks its own tokens. The cache stays rectangular: stock paged-attention kernels, vLLM's shared block table and HF's DynamicCache all work unchanged. This removes the ragged per-head paging that Tangram, KVzap and DMS need. Stored keys are post-RoPE at their original positions, so kernels need no per-head position indices. Keep logical positions; never renumber survivors.

The cost is losing per-head budget adaptivity (Ada-KV style). That matters at high compression, not at the 2-4x this piece targets.

### 3.4 Scorer v1 (training-free)
Blocks B = 64, lookahead L in {64, 256, 1024}. When block b is finalized, score each of its tokens per KV head by the attention it receives from the L lookahead queries (SnapKV's signal with a fixed causal window), max-pooled. Keep k per block, same k for every head and layer. Always keep sinks. An Expected-Attention-style variant (calibrated query statistics, no lookahead needed) is the second scorer and may be stronger because plain window scoring collapses at high compression (Expected Attention 65.4 vs SnapKV 19.2 on RULER-4K at 90% compression, query excluded).

### 3.5 The limit
Prefix-closed compression cannot see the future question, so it is query-agnostic by construction. The minimax analysis (arXiv 2607.01520) shows query-agnostic error scales with the full trace of the key covariance and that sharp lookup contexts compressed to half size can incur maximal error. The owner's own experiments confirmed it: every query-agnostic sampler scored near 0 on RULER needle tasks at 25% kept. Therefore the eviction piece targets **2-4x on shared prefixes**, not 10x. Higher compression is piece 2's job.

