## 4. Piece 2: don't evict, index and offload

### 4.1 Design
- Full KV lives in host (CPU) memory, in pages.
- The GPU holds a compact **index**: the CrossCov-U rank-r projection of each key (r = 32, bf16). For an 8B GQA model at 128K tokens this is about 2 GB, versus roughly 16 GB for the full KV.
- Per decode step: project the query, score it against the index, select the top tokens **once per GQA group** (not per query head, so the fetch set is shared), fetch the selected pages over PCIe, run attention on the fetched set.
- Amortization: compute selections at a few layers and reuse for adjacent layers (IndexCache 2603.12201 removes 75% of indexer work this way); reuse the previous step's selection and repair misses (PIVOT 2607.24593, Predict-Reuse-Repair 2606.30389).
- Nothing is evicted, so the design is **reversible**; the question is known at decode time, so there is no query-agnostic floor.

### 4.2 Why CrossCov-U finally pays here
On a GPU-resident cache, a better projection saves nothing: the full cache still occupies HBM, and the scan, top-k and gather still run. With the cache offloaded, the cost per step is **bytes fetched over PCIe plus a small GPU index scan**. Selection quality then sets speed: every token the index wrongly skips is a miss (accuracy loss or a repair fetch), every token it wrongly fetches is wasted bandwidth. Higher recall at a fixed rank means fewer bytes per step at the same accuracy. SAKI's 0.748 to 0.799 recall gain at r=32 is the size of headroom to expect from a score-aware basis.

### 4.3 The index is prefix-closed for free
Each token's projection depends only on its own key and a fixed calibrated basis. So index blocks are a deterministic function of the token prefix and can live in the radix tree, shared across requests, with no extra design. Piece 2 therefore meets both adoption criteria on its own: prefix compatibility and reversibility.

### 4.4 The combined system
Prefill: compute KV, write full KV pages to host memory, compute index blocks, store index blocks in the radix tree. Optionally apply the prefix-closed eviction of piece 1 to the GPU-resident hot set (recent tokens and sinks) so the resident footprint is rectangular and shareable. Decode: index scan, group-shared selection, page fetch, attention. Cost model: bytes fetched per step + index scan, versus the dense baseline's HBM reads.

