## 5. Prior art and positioning (must cite)

| Paper | What it already has | Difference |
|---|---|---|
| IntentKV 2606.09916 | Eviction that keeps radix matching alive (20.7% hits) | Masks slots, does not free memory; scores depend on session memory and the current query, so not prefix-closed |
| FlowKV 2505.15347 | Never re-compresses earlier turns | Turn-level only, one conversation, no engine |
| KVzap 2601.07891 | Causal per-token surrogate with fixed threshold (prefix-closed by construction) | Never mentions prefix caching; ragged per-head lengths; trained surrogate |
| DMS 2506.05345 | Decision at creation + fixed delay; per-head pages | Needs retrofit training; ragged; no sharing |
| KVzip 2505.23416 | Compress once, reuse across queries | Global top-r%, not closed; one fixed boundary; ~2x prefill cost |
| Tangram 2606.06302 | Ragged per-head paging on vLLM | Never mentions prefix caching; offline calibration; not released |
| HiSparse 2608.07009 | Full KV in host memory, GPU hot set | Per-request, per-layer cache; no cross-request sharing; radix cache disabled in its example |
| ShadowKV 2410.21465 | Low-rank pre-RoPE keys on GPU, values on CPU, landmark selection, ~3x throughput | Plain low-rank keys; no score-aware basis; no sharing property |
| Fathom 2609.17652 | Offloaded index with adaptive read depth, 1.67x over Loki-class scans | The rival index; compare head to head |
| SAKI 2608.03228 | Score-aware covariance-weighted projection, recall only | The projection baseline for CrossCov-U; no runtime |
| Loki 2406.02542 | Low-rank key scoring | No KV memory reduction; top-k bottleneck stated as open |
| Context-intensive failure study 2604.08426 | Low-rank keys and landmarks degrade on context-intensive tasks | The evaluation risk; include those tasks |
| Risk of KV compression 2607.01520 | Query-agnostic floor | Explains why piece 1 stops at 2-4x |
| TensorRT-LLM docs; vLLM RFCs #2614, #12254; SGLang unified radix cache and HiSparse blogs | What engines actually do | The motivation |

What is new: the named property and the hit-equals-miss proposition; head-uniform block selection on stock kernels; the first cross-request measurements of a prefix-closed scorer with prefix caching on; a score-aware index in the offloaded regime with the sharing property. The reviewer attack will be "DMS or KVzap-threshold plus a sliding-window radix component" for piece 1 and "ShadowKV with a different projection" for piece 2. The answers are the proposition, the stock-kernel design, the cross-request numbers, and bytes-per-step at matched accuracy.

Scoop risk is high: IntentKV, Leyline, Tangram, HiSparse and the SGLang unified radix cache all landed June-August 2026, and NVIDIA owns DMS, KVzap and TensorRT-LLM. An engine PR combining threshold eviction with prefix caching may exist; search vLLM, SGLang and TensorRT-LLM issues and PRs in the first session.

