## 8. Paper framing

Venue: MLSys, or NeurIPS/ICLR with the systems angle. One-line claim: "A KV cache that engines can share and recover: prefix-closed, reversible, on stock kernels, with a score-aware index that makes offloading fast."

Contributions in order: (1) the prefix-closure property and the hit-equals-miss proposition; (2) a head-uniform block design that runs on stock paged kernels; (3) a score-aware GPU index over an offloaded cache that is prefix-closed by construction, with bytes-per-step as the metric; (4) cross-request measurements with prefix caching on, which no paper has reported.

Framings to avoid: "we invented low-rank key indexing" (Loki, ShadowKV, SAKI); "our projection is better" as the headline (SAKI owns that; the headline is the system); "lossless" for piece 1 (it is exact for hits versus misses, not lossless versus a full cache).

